-- Q3829: IT General Controls [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Program change management and version control","System capacity planning and performance monitoring","Batch job scheduling and processing controls","Logical access controls and data protection"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per NIST SP 800-53 Rev. 5, control families AC (Access Control) and SC (System and Communications Protection), DLP controls address the logical access and data protection objective within IT general controls. DLP monitors data in motion, at rest, and in use to prevent unauthorized transmission of sensitive information across email, USB, and cloud channels.
Wrong (A): Program change management governs software modifications and version control, not monitoring or restricting data transfers.
Wrong (B): System capacity planning addresses resource utilization and performance thresholds, not data exfiltration prevention.
Wrong (C): Batch job scheduling controls the timing and sequencing of automated processing, unrelated to data loss prevention.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3829;

-- Q3831: IT General Controls [hard/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Automated processes will fail because passwords have technically expired","Service accounts will lock automatically after the defined inactivity period","The organization faces immediate regulatory fines for password policy violations","Stale credentials increase compromise risk, especially if exposed in a prior breach"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per NIST SP 800-63B and AICPA TSC CC6.1, service accounts with static, unchanging passwords create an indefinite exposure window. If credentials were compromised in a prior breach, the attacker retains persistent access until the password is eventually rotated. Organizations should use automated rotation or managed service identities.
Wrong (A): Service account passwords typically do not expire automatically unless a specific policy enforces expiration, so processes would not simply stop.
Wrong (B): Inactivity lockout applies to interactive user accounts, not service accounts that run automated processes continuously.
Wrong (C): Regulatory fines are not an immediate or automatic consequence of stale passwords; the primary risk is unauthorized access.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3831;

