UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["An IT balanced scorecard measuring performance across perspectives aligned with strategy","A chart of accounts used for general ledger classification and reporting","An employee satisfaction survey measuring workplace engagement levels","A physical inventory count of all IT hardware assets on premises"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under COBIT 2019 MEA01 (Managed Performance and Conformance Monitoring), an IT balanced scorecard measures governance performance across multiple perspectives: business contribution, user orientation, operational excellence, and future orientation. This tool links IT metrics directly to strategic business objectives for holistic assessment.
Wrong (B): A chart of accounts is a financial accounting tool for ledger classification, not an IT governance alignment assessment.
Wrong (C): An employee satisfaction survey measures workforce engagement, which does not evaluate IT governance alignment with business objectives.
Wrong (D): A physical inventory of hardware tracks IT assets but does not assess how well governance practices align with strategic goals.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3843;

-- Q3845: IT Audit Frameworks [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The objective is fully met because the preventive RBAC control alone satisfies access restriction","The auditor cannot evaluate until the organization completes retroactive access reviews","The objective is partially met since missing detective controls leave access changes unmonitored","The gap has no impact because detective controls are optional and do not affect objective achievement"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per AT-C Section 205.35, evaluating control objectives requires assessing both design adequacy and operating effectiveness. While RBAC limits initial access, the missing quarterly access review means unauthorized changes during two quarters may have gone undetected, weakening the overall control environment supporting the objective.
Wrong (A): A single preventive control does not fully satisfy an objective when complementary detective controls are specified but not performed.
Wrong (B): The auditor can and must evaluate the objective based on available evidence; waiting for retroactive reviews is not required.
Wrong (D): Detective controls are integral to control objectives, not optional supplements; their absence creates monitoring gaps that affect the assessment.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3845;

-- Q3847: IT Audit Frameworks [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Plan, which establishes shared vision, current status, and improvement direction for services","Engage, which provides stakeholder needs understanding, transparency, and continual engagement","Incident Resolution, which is a management practice rather than a value chain activity","Improve, which ensures continual improvement of products, services, and all value chain practices"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per ITIL 4 Foundation, the six service value chain activities are Plan, Improve, Engage, Design and Transition, Obtain/Build, and Deliver and Support. Incident Resolution is not among them; it falls under the incident management practice, which is one of 34 ITIL 4 management practices separate from the value chain.
Wrong (A): Plan is a legitimate value chain activity that establishes shared understanding of vision and improvement direction across all dimensions.
Wrong (B): Engage is a legitimate value chain activity that maintains stakeholder relationships and ensures transparency in service delivery.
Wrong (D): Improve is a legitimate value chain activity that drives continual enhancement of products, services, and practices.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3847;

-- Q3853: IT Audit Frameworks [medium/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["All IT areas receive equal audit attention regardless of their assessed risk level","The audit scope is determined randomly to ensure the auditor maintains independence","Only IT areas that failed in the previous audit cycle are included in scope","Higher-risk areas receive more audit attention while lower-risk areas receive less coverage"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per COBIT 2019 MEA03 and ISACA ITAF Standard 1202, a risk-based audit approach allocates resources based on assessed risk levels. Areas with higher inherent risk, greater control risk, or significant prior findings receive more intensive procedures, ensuring effort focuses where it provides the most value.
Wrong (A): Equal attention regardless of risk ignores the core principle of risk-based auditing, which prioritizes effort by risk level.
Wrong (B): Random scope determination is not a recognized audit methodology and would undermine the efficiency of resource allocation.
Wrong (C): Limiting scope to prior failures ignores newly emerging risks and areas that have not yet been audited.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3853;

-- Q3854: IT Audit Frameworks [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Plan, Protect, Detect, and Respond domains","Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond, and Recover functions","APO, BAI, DSS, and MEA management domains","Strategy, Design, Transition, and Operation phases"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): COBIT 2019 defines four management domains: APO (Align, Plan and Organize), BAI (Build, Acquire and Implement), DSS (Deliver, Service and Support), and MEA (Monitor, Evaluate and Assess). Together with the governance domain EDM, these five domains cover all 40 governance and management objectives.
Wrong (A): Plan, Protect, Detect, Respond is not a recognized framework domain structure; it conflates elements from multiple frameworks.
Wrong (B): Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond, Recover are the five functions of the NIST Cybersecurity Framework, not COBIT 2019 domains.
Wrong (D): Strategy, Design, Transition, Operation are lifecycle stages from ITIL v3, not COBIT 2019 management domains.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3854;

-- Q3855: IT Audit Frameworks [medium/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A recurring activity at all levels ensuring alignment with changing business needs","A one-time assessment of IT services with all changes implemented at once","An annual IT audit with results filed to regulatory authorities","A scheduled replacement of all IT systems every three years"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ITIL 4 Foundation, continual improvement is a core practice that operates at all organizational levels to ensure services remain aligned with changing business needs. It uses the ITIL continual improvement model and a continual improvement register (CIR) to track and prioritize improvement opportunities systematically.
Wrong (B): A one-time assessment contradicts the ongoing, iterative nature of continual improvement, which is embedded in daily operations.
Wrong (C): An annual audit filed with regulators is a compliance activity, not the ITIL practice of continual improvement.
Wrong (D): Scheduled wholesale technology replacement is a capital planning approach, not the incremental and transformational improvement ITIL describes.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3855;

-- Q3857: IT Audit Frameworks [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Interrelated components including processes, structures, policies, and culture governing IT","A single software application that automates all governance activities","A written document that describes the organization''s IT policies","The IT department''s organizational chart and reporting structure"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per COBIT 2019 Framework Section 3 (Governance System and Components), a governance system is the set of interrelated components — processes, organizational structures, policies, information flows, culture, skills, and services — that work together to govern and manage enterprise IT, tailored through design factors to the organization''s context.
Wrong (B): A single software application cannot encompass the full scope of a governance system, which includes people, culture, and organizational structures.
Wrong (C): A policy document is only one component within the broader governance system, not the system itself.
Wrong (D): An organizational chart is merely one structural element; the governance system integrates multiple components beyond reporting lines.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3857;

-- Q3859: SOC Testing Controls [easy/L4]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Whether controls operated effectively throughout the examination period","Whether user entities have implemented their complementary controls","Whether the service organization''s financial statements are fairly presented","Whether controls are suitably designed to meet applicable control objectives"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per AT-C Section 320, tests of design evaluate whether controls, if operating as described, would be suitably designed to achieve the related control objectives or meet the applicable Trust Services Criteria. Design testing determines if the control is capable of effectively addressing the identified risk.
Wrong (A): Testing whether controls operated effectively describes tests of operating effectiveness, which are separate from and performed after design tests.
Wrong (B): Evaluating complementary user entity controls is the responsibility of user entities and their auditors, not the service auditor''s design testing.
Wrong (C): Fair presentation of financial statements is the objective of a financial statement audit, not a SOC engagement''s design testing.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3859;

-- Q3860: SOC Testing Controls [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Inquiry of management, inspection of documentation, and observation","Independent reperformance of the control by the auditor","Statistical sampling of the transaction population for deviations","Reconciliation of account balances to supporting documentation"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per AT-C Section 320, testing control design typically involves inquiry of management, inspection of relevant documentation such as policies and system configurations, and observation of the control being performed. These procedures help the auditor understand how the control is designed and whether it addresses the identified risk.
Wrong (B): Reperformance is used to test operating effectiveness by independently re-executing the control, not to evaluate design suitability.
Wrong (C): Statistical sampling tests populations of transactions for deviations during operating effectiveness testing, not during design evaluation.
Wrong (D): Reconciliation of account balances is a financial audit procedure unrelated to evaluating IT control design in SOC engagements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3860;

-- Q3864: SOC Testing Controls [medium/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Selecting all items in the population for complete testing","Selecting a sample and testing each item for a specific characteristic","Calculating the average dollar value of transactions in the period","Testing only the largest transactions based on monetary amount"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AT-C Section 205 and AICPA Audit Sampling guidance, attribute sampling involves selecting a representative sample and examining each item for the presence or absence of a specific characteristic, such as documented approval. The results estimate the deviation rate in the entire population, producing a binary pass/fail outcome per item.
Wrong (A): Selecting all items describes census testing, not attribute sampling, which tests a subset to draw population-level conclusions.
Wrong (C): Calculating average dollar values describes variable sampling used in financial audits, not attribute sampling for control compliance.
Wrong (D): Testing only the largest transactions describes directed sampling by monetary amount, not the representative approach of attribute sampling.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3864;

-- Q3865: SOC Testing Controls [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A higher expected deviation rate or lower tolerable deviation rate","An automated control that operates consistently without variation","A control that operates only once per year during the period","A very small population of items available for testing"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per the AICPA Guide: SOC 2 Reporting on Controls, larger sample sizes are required when the expected deviation rate is higher or the tolerable deviation rate is lower. Prior exceptions increase the expected deviation rate, and critical controls demand tighter tolerance, both driving larger samples for adequate assurance.
Wrong (B): Automated controls with consistent execution typically require smaller samples because reduced variability means fewer items need testing.
Wrong (C): Annual controls have only one occurrence to test; this reduces sample size to one, the opposite of increasing it.
Wrong (D): A very small population limits the maximum possible sample and may call for census testing rather than a larger statistical sample.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3865;

-- Q3866: SOC Testing Controls [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["To test operating effectiveness over the entire examination period","To understand the control by tracing a transaction end to end","To calculate the error rate in the overall transaction population","To evaluate the service organization''s financial performance"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AT-C Section 320, a walkthrough involves tracing a single transaction or event through the entire process from initiation to completion. The purpose is to confirm the auditor''s understanding of the process, verify that described controls are in place, and identify where controls operate within the workflow.
Wrong (A): Walkthroughs trace a single transaction and do not provide evidence of operating effectiveness over the full examination period.
Wrong (C): Calculating error rates requires statistical sampling of multiple items, not the single-transaction trace of a walkthrough.
Wrong (D): Evaluating financial performance falls outside SOC engagement scope and is unrelated to walkthrough procedures.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3866;

-- Q3867: SOC Testing Controls [medium/L4]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Ignore the exceptions because the majority of items were properly approved","Automatically issue a qualified opinion because any exception requires qualification","Evaluate the nature, cause, and frequency of exceptions to assess systemic impact","Request management to retroactively approve the three changes to clear exceptions"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per the AICPA Guide: SOC 2 Reporting on Controls, the auditor must evaluate each exception by assessing its nature, cause, frequency, and potential impact on the control objective before determining the effect on the opinion. Three exceptions out of 25 (12% deviation rate) may indicate a systemic weakness requiring further investigation.
Wrong (A): Ignoring exceptions violates professional standards requiring the auditor to evaluate all identified deviations regardless of the overall pass rate.
Wrong (B): A qualified opinion is not automatic; the auditor must first assess whether exceptions represent isolated incidents or systemic control failure.
Wrong (D): Retroactive approvals do not remediate the control failure; the changes were deployed without approval at the time, which is the relevant finding.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3867;

-- Q3869: SOC Testing Controls [medium/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Sampling-based testing of multiple control instances","Benchmarking or baseline testing of the automated configuration","Full population testing of all control occurrences","Risk-based testing prioritizing high-impact control areas"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AICPA SOC examination guidance (AT-C Section 320), benchmarking is a testing approach for automated controls where the auditor verifies the control operates as configured at a point in time, then confirms through change management records that the configuration remained unchanged during the examination period. This leverages the inherent consistency of automated controls.
Wrong (A): Sampling-based testing selects multiple instances for evaluation, which is unnecessary when the automated configuration has not changed.
Wrong (C): Full population testing examines every occurrence and is used for frequently operating controls, not for verifying static automated configurations.
Wrong (D): Risk-based testing describes an audit planning methodology for prioritizing areas, not a specific technique for verifying automated control configurations.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3869;

-- Q3873: SOC Testing Controls [hard/L4]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The control operated effectively because all four quarterly reviews were completed on schedule","The auditor should test only the March review since it has the strongest supporting evidence","The control was not properly designed and the organization must redesign it entirely","The control may lack effectiveness because three reviews lack follow-up evidence on findings"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per AT-C Section 320.35 and AICPA TSC CC6.1, an effective access review must include not only the periodic review itself but also documented evidence of follow-up and remediation of identified issues. Three of four quarters lacking follow-up documentation suggests the control did not operate effectively throughout the examination period.
Wrong (A): Completing the reviews alone is insufficient; operating effectiveness requires documented follow-up on issues identified during each review.
Wrong (B): Testing only one quarter ignores the requirement to evaluate operating effectiveness across the entire examination period.
Wrong (C): The control design is adequate since quarterly reviews with follow-up are appropriate; the issue is operating effectiveness, not design.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3873;

-- Q3876: SOC Testing Controls [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Inquiry, because the auditor is asking the tool to report on encryption status","Inspection, because the auditor examines actual configuration against documented policy","Reperformance, because the auditor re-executes the encryption configuration process","Observation, because the auditor watches the scanning tool analyze settings"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AT-C Section 320.39 (SSAE No. 18), using a scanning tool to extract and examine actual system configuration settings and compare them against the documented encryption policy constitutes inspection. The auditor is examining existing system state and comparing it to established criteria, providing direct objective evidence of control operation.
Wrong (A): Inquiry involves asking management questions about configurations; running a scanning tool produces direct evidence rather than relying on representations.
Wrong (C): Reperformance involves independently re-executing the control itself; the scanning tool reads existing settings rather than reconfiguring encryption.
Wrong (D): Observation involves watching personnel perform a process in real time; the auditor is examining output data, not watching a human process.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3876;

-- Q3877: SOC Testing Controls [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Haphazard selection without a structured methodology","Systematic selection using a fixed interval and random start","Representative sampling to evaluate an attribute across the population","Census testing of every item in the full population"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per AT-C Section 320.40 and the AICPA Audit Sampling Guide, selecting items from a population and testing them for specific attributes such as documented manager approval constitutes representative sampling. The deviation rate observed in the sample is projected to draw conclusions about the likely deviation rate in the entire population.
Wrong (A): Haphazard selection describes only an item selection method and lacks the structured representative approach needed for attribute testing conclusions.
Wrong (B): Systematic selection is a sampling technique for choosing items, not a description of the overall attribute testing procedure being performed.
Wrong (D): Census testing examines every item in the population; selecting 25 from 1,200 is sampling, not a complete population test.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3877;

-- Q3879: SOC Testing Controls [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["25 or more items, based on the auditor''s risk assessment","1 item, since only a single day needs to be tested","5 items, representing one sample per quarter plus one","All 365 daily occurrences in the examination period"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per the AICPA Guide: SOC 2 Reporting on Controls (Appendix B), a minimum sample of 25 items is recommended for daily controls over a 12-month Type II examination period. The auditor may increase the sample based on risk assessment and expected deviation rate to achieve sufficient statistical confidence.
Wrong (B): Testing only 1 item provides no statistical basis for drawing conclusions about 365 daily control occurrences throughout the year.
Wrong (C): A sample of 5 is insufficient for daily controls; it does not meet minimum professional guidance thresholds for reliable conclusions.
Wrong (D): Testing all 365 occurrences is census testing, which exceeds the minimum requirement and is not the typical minimum sample size.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3879;

-- Q3881: SOC Testing Controls [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Inquiry of knowledgeable personnel about the process","Inspection with corroboration between two independent sources","Observation of the change management process being performed","Walkthrough of a single change request end to end"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AT-C Section 205.29, inspection involves examining records and documents to obtain audit evidence. Comparing the production change log against the change management system verifies documented approvals through dual-source corroboration, which provides more reliable evidence than a single source.
Wrong (A): Inquiry involves asking personnel questions; the auditor is independently examining records from two systems rather than relying on verbal representations.
Wrong (C): Observation involves watching a process being performed in real time, not cross-referencing documentation from two separate sources after the fact.
Wrong (D): A walkthrough traces a single transaction end to end; cross-referencing an entire log against a system is systematic inspection, not a walkthrough.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3881;

-- Q3882: SOC Testing Controls [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A sample of 25 occurrences from the examination period","No testing is required for controls performed annually","Every occurrence within the period, which is one for annual controls","A minimum of 10 occurrences regardless of control frequency"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per SSAE 18 (AT-C Section 320.34), for controls that operate infrequently such as annually, the service auditor must test every occurrence within the examination period. For an annual control with only one occurrence, that single instance must be tested in its entirety rather than sampled.
Wrong (A): A sample of 25 applies to frequently occurring controls like daily or weekly; an annual control has only one occurrence to test.
Wrong (B): Annual controls still require testing; no control within the engagement scope is exempt from evaluation regardless of frequency.
Wrong (D): A minimum of 10 is not a recognized threshold; sample sizes depend on control frequency, and annual controls require testing all occurrences.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3882;

-- Q3887: SOC Reporting [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Auditor''s report, management''s assertion, system description, and tests with results","Only the auditor''s report and a brief summary conclusion paragraph","The service organization''s independently audited financial statements","The user entity''s internal audit findings and recommendations"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per SSAE 18 (AT-C Section 320), a SOC 1 Type II report contains four main sections: the independent service auditor''s report (opinion), management''s assertion about the system and controls, the system description prepared by management, and the description of tests of controls and results thereof.
Wrong (B): A SOC 1 Type II report includes more than just the opinion and a summary; management''s assertion, system description, and test results are all required sections.
Wrong (C): Financial statements are not part of a SOC 1 report; the report addresses internal controls over financial reporting at the service organization.
Wrong (D): User entity internal audit findings are produced by the user entity, not included in the service organization''s SOC 1 report.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3887;

-- Q3888: SOC Reporting [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Fair presentation, suitable design, and for Type II, operating effectiveness","The service auditor''s testing methodology and planned sample sizes","The user entity''s satisfaction with the service organization''s performance","The competitive position of the service organization in its industry"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per AT-C Section 320, management''s assertion is a written statement in which the service organization''s management asserts that the system description fairly presents the system, that controls are suitably designed, and (for Type II) that controls operated effectively throughout the specified period. The service auditor''s opinion evaluates this assertion.
Wrong (B): Testing methodology and sample sizes are the auditor''s professional decisions, not subjects of management''s assertion.
Wrong (C): User entity satisfaction is outside the scope of the SOC engagement and is not addressed in management''s assertion.
Wrong (D): The organization''s competitive position is a business strategy matter unrelated to the SOC report''s management assertion.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3888;

-- Q3889: SOC Reporting [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The report includes a complete opinion on the subservice organization''s controls","The report identifies carved-out functions and the opinion excludes those controls","The report excludes any mention of the subservice organization entirely","The report automatically includes a qualified opinion due to the carve-out"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per SSAE 18 (AT-C Section 320.A27), the carve-out method excludes the subservice organization''s controls from the service auditor''s scope. The report identifies the subservice organization and describes its services, but the opinion does not extend to its controls. User entities must evaluate the subservice organization independently.
Wrong (A): The carve-out method specifically excludes the subservice organization''s controls from the scope; including a complete opinion would require the inclusive method.
Wrong (C): The report must identify and describe the subservice organization and its functions, even though its controls are excluded from the opinion scope.
Wrong (D): A carve-out does not automatically result in a qualified opinion; it is a scoping decision, not an indication of a control deficiency.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3889;
